THOUGHT: NullAway complains that the constructor doesn’t guarantee non-null initialization of iterator1 and iterator2. They are lazily initialized in iterator(), but the fields are likely annotated or inferred as @NonNull. A minimal fix is to eagerly initialize them in the constructor so they are always non-null, while preserving existing lazy logic (which will just reuse them). I’ll modify the constructor to create the two QueueIterator instances there.

```bash
cd /home/vscode/nullness-benchmarks/libgdx && sed -i '560,563c\    public QueueIterable(Queue<T> queue, boolean allowRemove) {
      this.queue = queue;
      this.allowRemove = allowRemove;
      this.iterator1 = new QueueIterator(queue, allowRemove);
      this.iterator2 = new QueueIterator(queue, allowRemove);
    }' gdx/src/com/badlogic/gdx/utils/Queue.java && ./gradlew gdx:compileJava -x test -q > new_build_output.log 2>&1 && grep -E "NullAway:|error: NullAway" -n new_build_output.log | tail -n 5 && grep -E "NullAway.*errors" -n new_build_output.log | tail -n 3```

